WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.129 --> 00:00:06.540
Te electric knife fish or electric eel
to its friends, has one of the most

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:12.210
shocking talents in the animal kingdom,
literally. Used to sense and attack prey

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:17.100
the electric eel's ability to generate
store and release jolts from within its

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.529
body has fascinated scientists for
centuries, and now they're mimicking it

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:27.599
on a quest to create a flexible bio-battery which could power all kinds of

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:33.980
futuristic tech, to wear on our bodies
and even inside our bodies.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.160
To make an electric eel inspired battery
the first thing to understand is how the

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.370
eels do it. Enter specialised cells
called electrocytes. These cells have

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:55.200
membranes that allow certain charged
ions to pass through, leaving the cell in

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.420
a neutral state when it's resting. When
the eel is ready to strike, channels on

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.430
one side of the cell open and positive
ions rush in generating a current and

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.630
zap! It's curtains for the unsuspecting
prey.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:14.729
Each electrocyte only produces around
150 millivolts, but large eels can have

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:19.560
thousands of them packed together like a
battery. Using some clever nervous system

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:27.110
timing they're able to activate them all
simultaneously making for a hefty shock.

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:31.890
Scientists based at the University of
Freiburg in Switzerland took inspiration

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.780
from this process to create an
artificial electric organ They used

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.970
blobs of hydrogel to mimic each part of
the electrocyte - yellow and green blobs

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:46.470
act as the selectively permeable
membranes, red and blue blobs contain

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:52.320
different levels of the ions. Just like
the eel, ions rush across these membranes

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.210
to generate a current. Each group of four
blobs can produce about the same voltage

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:03.180
as an electric eel's electrocyte. Also
like the eel scientists wanted to stack

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.459
their artificial cells up to increase
the voltage, but how do you do that? One

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:12.840
answer: printing. When laid on top of each
other

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.239
these two sheets, laden with hydrogel
blobs, form one long line of repeating

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:22.360
units - enough to generate about a hundred volts.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.370
Now there's one more thing; the eel fires
all of its electrocytes simultaneously

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.950
to get maximum voltage and the
scientists need to do the same. To get

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:38.230
that high voltage all of the gels need
to contact at the same time. The sheets

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.460
made it faster but for truly
simultaneous connections the researchers

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:48.310
came up with another idea: folding. This
allowed all the biogel blobs to touch

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:54.820
each other almost simultaneously. The eel
can recharge its battery biologically

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.560
within its body, whereas right now the
artificial battery needs to be connected

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.690
up to a power source. Researchers want to
use their battery to power things like

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:10.450
contact lenses with integrated displays,
or biological sensors or even wearable

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.830
tech, so the hope is that their battery
too will one day be recharged

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:22.350
biologically. After all if the eel can do
it, why can't we? Right

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.280
 

